  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Role
    
    The Gore Emissary is a Wake-Singer delegate who attends Nightshelf diplomatic sessions, entering through the flooded access channels that represent the only passages in penguin architecture large enough to accommodate an orca body. She wears a headdress of carved ancestor-bone — a liturgical marker whose significance penguin commentators routinely misread as decorative. Her voice registers subsonic, felt in the sternum before the ears process it. An escort of at least two larger orcas holds position in the lower approaches during sessions. Their presence is formally recorded as ceremonial. It is not ceremonial.
    
    ## The Junction Twelve Session
    
    The primary documented account places the Gore Emissary at a quarterly fire-core certification session at Junction Twelve beneath Midway Shelf, seated in a shallow pool carved for orca delegates. She remained silent through the opening formalities while the Cell's trade-speaker recited production figures. When she spoke, she observed that the eastern thermal vents of ☽'slew Ravium~ — the deep warren then contested between the Cell and the Midnight Claws — remained unmonitored. The statement's grammar permitted it to be read as accusation or offer. She did not clarify.
    
    What the official minutes do not record is that she was listening not to the figures but to the stone, using Junction Twelve's acoustics to map resonance patterns from every branching tunnel within sixty meters. Orca echolocation applied to penguin architecture. The implications require no elaboration.
    
    ## Private Negotiations
    
    In a separate exchange with Niaecum~ of ☽'ofund' Guild, the Gore Emissary abandoned the hunt-metaphors that constitute formal orca diplomatic register. When Niaecum~ relayed that the Cell had tracked Wake-Singer movements below the fourth depth — intelligence designed to provoke the Emissary into revealing which routes she already knew — the Gore Emissary identified the maneuver aloud.
    
    She warned that the corruption-scar at Ravium's center was not a resource but a containment problem, and that the Cell was, in her phrasing, *pulling at its teeth*. She accepted a bone-marked tablet of ☽'ofund' tunnel cartography from Niaecum~, requesting only verification that one route led where claimed. Her chanters then began singing those passage geometries into Wake-Singer oral memory — knowledge that, once committed to orca tradition, no guild vote or parliamentary review could extract.
    
    The alliance bypassed both the Cell and the Midnight Claws. The Cell responded by sealing corridors and cutting fire-core supply. Whether this cost them more than it cost Niaecum~ remains an open question. What is not open is that the Wake-Singers now carry penguin tunnel architecture in their songs.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771594740819_0", anchor: "every branching tunnel within sixty meters", text: "Sixty meters. Who measured? The official minutes do not record the echolocation. ☽'ofund' Guild's post-session intelligence brief — if that is the source — is acoustic reconstruction, not observation. The number has the smell of someone working backward from known tunnel geometries to produce a range estimate that looks like measurement. I let it stand because the principle is sound. The precision is not.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771594740819_1", anchor: "a headdress of carved ancestor-bone — a liturgical marker whose significance penguin commentators routinely misread as decorative", text: "Wake-Singer 'ancestor-bone' is not ancestral in the penguin sense. The carvings are consecrated kill-records — prey offered to the dead god, scrimshawed with hunt-geometries that double as liturgical notation. Every bone in that headdress is someone who was eaten. Wearing it into a negotiation chamber is not ceremony. It is a curated inventory of what cooperation with the Wake-Singers has historically cost, displayed on the body of the delegate asking you to cooperate again. That penguin commentators read this as ornamentation is itself a data point about how badly we need orca-literate diplomatic staff.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771594740819_2", anchor: "knowledge that, once committed to orca tradition, no guild vote or parliamentary review could extract", text: "Every orca raid documented on the Berg follows shore-geometry: breach through ice, herd along surface, finish in water. Tunnels invert this. Underground, penguins hold every advantage — narrow passages neutralize size, branching routes defeat herding, there is no water below the escape route. That was the arithmetic that kept the Nightshelf alive. The Wake-Singers now carry the map that solves it. Niaecum~ handed them the key to the one place penguins could not be hunted and called it an alliance.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771594740819_3", anchor: "The Cell responded by sealing corridors and cutting fire-core supply", text: "The Cell's last documented act of institutional will. They disappear from the record after ☽'slew Ravium~ changed hands. Sealing corridors against an enemy that has just memorized your architecture in song is not strategy. It is panic formatted as policy.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
